  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "Low-frequency noise has a variety of adverse physiological and psychological effects on humans.",
    "The adverse effects of low-frequency noise are often more serious than those produced by higher frequency noise, due to its pervasiveness, efficient propagation, and reduced efficacy of many structures in attenuating it.",
    "Concerned action is warranted based on current evidence, without waiting for potentially lengthy delays for definitive proof.",
    "More emphasis should be placed on determining the frequency spectrum of a noise rather than solely focusing on sound-pressure level.",
    "Standards should consider allowing less noise in the low-frequency range, as a stimulus may have an effect even without conscious (auditory) detection.",
    "Low-frequency noise emission should be actively reduced through insulation of the source, better maintenance of machinery, or active sound absorption."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Too little research on the role of different frequency spectra of noise in producing effects on humans.",
    "Most research used short durations; there is a need for studies with prolonged exposure (at least 15-30 min) to generalize laboratory findings to field situations.",
    "Longitudinal studies are needed to examine the long-term pattern of effects and identify predictors of clinical significance.",
    "Opportunities to assess noise effects when sources change substantially (e.g., new freeways, airports) should be utilized.",
    "Comparisons of different low-frequency noise sources are needed, with statistical control for confounding differences.",
    "Mechanisms of individual differences in noise effects, and features shared by affected individuals, need examination.",
    "The impact of environmental noise with low-frequency components on various risk groups (e.g., hearing impaired, noise sensitive, children, elderly) needs research.",
    "Standardized techniques to measure low-frequency noise in laboratory, housing, and work sites are desirable.",
    "Laboratory studies on the effects of various features of (real and artificial) noise signals are needed.",
    "Further examination of the relative contributions of low-frequency, impulsiveness, and tonal aspects of noise in laboratory and field studies.",
    "Detailed assessment of the relative importance of vibration and rattle versus the low-frequency noise itself in producing reactions.",
    "Continued development of methods for low-frequency noise attenuation and control measurement technology."
  ],
